IMO, that is similar to 

```js
var foo    = 1,
    barbaz = 2;
```

...which the rule already warns about. So I don't think it's necessary to put it behind an option, since if people prefer that behavior then they can just disable the rule.

@platinumazure Do you mean adding checking comments at all via new option, or inline comments at the end of the line, as @not-an-aardvark mentioned? I'm fine with that, though I do think the following should always warn:

```js
console.log('hello',     /* comment */ 'world');
console.log('hello', /* comment */     'world');
```
